  "strategy": "Apporach and rationale:\nOur approach improves upon the parent program by enhancing both initialization and optimization. The core idea is to combine several strategies that can better explore the sequence space and find candidates that lower the AC1 upper bound. We focus on using several powerful optimization methods from the previous program but with adaptive parameter tuning. We introduce a hybrid optimization strategy that sequentially applies:\n1. Linear Programming (LP) for initial coarse optimization\n2. Subgradient methods for precise refinement\n3. Coordinate descent with adaptive restarts for escaping local minima\nAdditionally, we incorporate dimension exploration by considering sequences of different lengths (including modifying the parent's length) and include restart strategies that periodically introduce significant changes to the candidate to prevent stagnation. This multi-stage approach allows us to improve the objective function effectively.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe seek a non-negative sequence a of length n (either derived from or different from the parent's length) with \u2211a \u2265 0.01 and a_i \u2264 1000 for all i that minimizes:\nf(a) = 2 * n * max_{k} (convolve(a, a)[k]) / (\u2211a)^2\n\nThis involves:\n- Variable: non-negative real numbers a = [a\u2080, ..., a_{n-1}]\n- Objective: minimize f(a)\n- Norm: L_\u221e for the convolution\n- Constraints: a_i \u2208 [0, 1000] for all i and \u2211a \u2265 0.01\n\nImp lementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Start from the parent sequence directly and use a relaxed LP initialization\n   - Generate diverse candidates by randomly puncturing and scaling the parent sequence\n   - For each candidate, project into L1 space and normalize to unit sum\n\n2. Optimization ordering:\n   a) Phase 1: LP-based coarse optimization (50% of budget)\n      - Formulate the LP to maximize \u2211b subject to ||f*g||_\u221e \u2264 M, where f is the current candidate\n      - Use 'highs' method with presolve enabled for efficiency\n      - Update candidate using a linear interpol between current and LP solution\n\n   b) Phase 2: Subgradient optimization refinement (30% of budget)\n      - Use analytic gradient computation from _subgrad_maxconv()\n      - Employ momentum and adaptive learning rates\n      - Include clipping of updates to maintain non-negativity\n\n   c) Phase 3: Adaptive coordinate descent (15% of budget)\n      - Select coordinates based on gradient magnitude\n      - Perform multidimensional scaling along axes\n      - Restart if improvement stalls\n\n   d) Dimensions and length changes (if time permits)\n      - If time remains, try nearby sequence lengths\n      - For each dimension L, use the parent as a stretched function and optimize\n\n3. Search schedule:\n   - Each optimization has a time limit\n   - Check time usage at least every 1 second\n   - Preserve the best candidate found\n   - Use early stopping if objective plateaus\n\n4. Unique variations:\n   - Recursive adaptive allocation of budget based on success rate\n   - Multi-point restarts: at random intervals, replace a fraction of the candidate \n   - Direction preservation: use gradient information to bias restarts\n   - Dimension sensitivity analysis: prefer dimensions based on previous objective values\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n   - Time estimation:\n     LP-based: O(0.5 * budget seconds)\n     Subgradient: O(0.3 * budget seconds)\n     Coordinate descent: O(0.15 * budget seconds)\n     Dimension: O(0.05 * budget seconds)\n   - Memory strategy: Use dimension-specific temporary allocations\n   - Solver management: Prioritize simplex for LP, trust-region for nonlinear\n   - Early termination: Final evaluation with exact evaluator\n\nValidation and return contract:\n   - Maintain separate storage for fallback candidate (normalized parent)\n   - Periodically evaluate best candidate with the task's uniform AC1 bound\n   - Ensure all proposed sequences are finite and nonnegative\n   - Return best candidate with lowest AC1 score\n\nImplementation summary:\n   - Create helper functions for each optimization phase\n   - Use vectorized operations from numpy where possible\n   - For convolution, rely on FFT for computational efficiency\n   - Maintain a log of scores for failure detection\n\nFallback strategy:\n   - Primary: initial candidate (normalized parent) of length 414\n   - Secondary: if main algorithm fails, use the fallback or initialize randomly\n\nCode structure:\n   - define_proposed_candidate(:\n      manage_budget()\n      define_initialization_methods()\n      define_optimization_stages()\n      logging_and_output_mechnism()\n\n   def manage_budget():\n      total_time = budget_s\n      time_phase1 = 0.45 * total_time\n      etc...\n\n   def define_initialization_methods():\n        ...\n\n   def define_optimization_stages():\n        ...\n\n   The best_candidate will be tracked throughout.",
